WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
 So welcome back, we're now going to talk about expressions.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.880
 Expressions are a little more complex calculations

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.360
 that we can sort of do on the right-hand side

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.720
 of an assignment statement.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.360
 So one of the things about expressions is operators.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.760
 And then operators in computer programming

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.560
 are often very much the same as the mathematical operators,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.320
 but we don't have all the fancy characters

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.360
 that we have in mathematics,

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.680
 and so we have to choose what's on the keyboard,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.200
 and then we really go back to the 1960s and 1970s,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.640
 and we used what was on the keyboard in the 1960s

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.120
 and the 1970s to make these operators.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.440
 So plus is addition, minus is subtraction.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.080
 We don't have a time sign or a dot in the middle,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.040
 so we use the asterisk as multiplication.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.280
 Division, we can't put two things over top of each other,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.280
 so we use slash for division.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.280
 Raising to the power,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.960
 because it didn't have little characters back then,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.960
 is star star, which is raising to the power.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.000
 And then remainder, remainder is the,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.600
 when you do integer division,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.400
 it's also called the modulo operator,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.160
 it's the remainder, not the quotient,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.660
 and I've got a picture of that coming up.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.720
 So here's a whole series of little examples of this, right?

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.200
 So we've already seen the plus, x equals x plus one.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.680
 Keep remembering that these assignments are arrows,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.400
 basically, arrow, arrow, they have a direction.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.160
 Multiplication, 440 times 12.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.080
 Dividing this by, that's division over 1000, 5.28.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.320
 Here we're gonna put 23 into JJ,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.280
 and then we're gonna do modulo.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.940
 So that says, take 23, divide it by five,

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.080
 and give me back the remainder and put it in KK.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.000
 So this is the expression that evaluates like this.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.840
 Take 23, divide five into 23, four, remainder three.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.840
 The three is what comes back up here, okay?

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.800
 And so that is the remainder.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.560
 It's also called modulo operator.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.840
 It turns out that for things like picking a random number

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.080
 and then taking the modulo of 52

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.920
 is a way to pick a card randomly.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.200
 So this modulo operator is actually,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.360
 especially in games and other things, super useful.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.440
 So that's the various operators.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.520
 It's important to know which of these operators goes first.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.480
 That's called operator precedence.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.960
 Now normally we put parentheses in,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.320
 like, you know, so if I put the parentheses in here,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.760
 I'd say this goes first, parentheses,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.600
 then this goes first, oops, actually not that one.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.840
 Oops, got that one wrong.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.840
 This happens first, this happens, then this happens, okay?

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.320
 And so, but it's important for us to be able to know

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.600
 if there were no parentheses,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.900
 the order in which these things will happen.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.480
 So the way things work in terms of operator precedence

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.180
 is parentheses are the most important thing,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.600
 followed by raising to the power, all else being equal,

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.240
 multiplication and division are both equal,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.840
 and then addition, and then within,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.200
 it's adding left to right.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.240
 So let's see an example of how this works.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.240
 And so if we take one plus two raised to the three power,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.200
 divided by four times five,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.320
 and we print out what comes out of this.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.920
 So the way I did this when I was taking exams back

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.880
 many, many years ago when I was first in computer science

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.000
 is I'd write it all down

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.640
 and I'd look for the highest precedence thing.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.600
 Now parentheses would make this easy,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.460
 but exponentiation is the first one.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.240
 So that means we're gonna take this,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:23.080
 and that's gonna be eight, two to the third power,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:28.080
 two times two times two, two cubed is eight.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.080
 Then what I would do is I rewrite the whole thing

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.160
 with the eight there, and now I look across,

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.920
 and I'm looking for multiplications,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.080
 'cause the power's been done,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.880
 the multiplication's what I'm looking for next.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.940
 And then there is both multiplication and division,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.080
 they're equal, they're at the same level.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.340
 And so what happens is they're done left to right.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.480
 Eight divided by four happens before four times five,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.920
 and so the fact that it's not four times five,

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.400
 but instead eight times four

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.840
 is because of the left to right rule.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.360
 So then this gets rewritten to be two,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.120
 one plus two times five,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.980
 and this one, multiplication is the top one.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.480
 So that does this next, two times five becomes 10,

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.120
 I rewrite it again, and then one plus 10 addition

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.240
 is the lowest thing, and that's how we end up with 11.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.660
 And so that's how I would do these problems

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.680
 if I ever saw the problem on an exam.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.860
 And it's a fun problem to put on exams

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.380
 because there is one and only one answer,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.900
 and every programming class has usually

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.240
 at least one slide about this stuff.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.740
 So like I said, the rules go top to bottom,

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.960
 parentheses, power, multiplication, addition,

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.600
 and then left to right within it.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.400
 So we've talked about variables and computing values

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.000
 to put inside variables,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.620
 but the one thing you've kinda also,

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.160
 maybe you noticed it as we go by,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.660
 is we have different kinds of data.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.020
 We call it type.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.260
 Is this of type integer?

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.300
 Is this of type floating point number?

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.580
 Is it of type string?

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.340
 What is going on here?

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.100
 And Python is pretty smart

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.300
 about various kinds of types of data.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.180
 And so we're adding one plus four here,

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.160
 and Python knows as it looks at this

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.060
 that that's an integer and that's an integer,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.980
 and we'll add it together and make it an integer.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.860
 So that thing is an integer.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.140
 We can also use this plus to concatenate two strings.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.980
 This is hello blank plus there.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.260
 And plus looks here says,

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.980
 oh, that's a string and that's a string.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.540
 So I know what to do with strings.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.420
 I will concatenate those two things together

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.020
 so it becomes another string

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.940
 that gets assigned into EEE and it's hello space there.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.340
 The plus doesn't add the space.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.100
 I added the space by putting it right there.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.060
 And so these operators are kinda smart

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.940
 in that they kinda know what they're dealing with

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.780
 and sometimes they will do one thing or another

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.140
 depending on the kinds of values,

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:42.420
 variables, or constants that they're working with.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.000
 And so sometimes type can get us in trouble.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.740
 So here we have EEE which is hello there

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.040
 because we've concatenated these two strings together.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.880
 And now we're adding one and the problem now

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.680
 is that it looks on one side and says that's a string

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.340
 and that's a number and says I don't know how to do that.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.420
 This is another one of those annoying errors

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.580
 that you think that somehow Python doesn't like you

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.380
 but it just is confused.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.380
 If you look at these things, trace back,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.420
 trace back always means I quit.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.040
 It means I stopped, I ran, I'm quitting now

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.260
 because I don't wanna go any farther

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.980
 because I've become confused.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:19.060
 So your program stops running and you say

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.420
 here's where I stopped running

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:21.620
 'cause we're typing interactively.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.180
 It's always line one here.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.260
 Type it, but you if you read carefully

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.380
 and you don't get too stuck on too much stuff,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.200
 line one that tells us something in module type error

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.460
 can't convert int object to stir implicitly.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.820
 So that's an integer right there and that's a string

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.340
 and that's what it's complaining about,

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:39.820
 that little bit right there.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.640
 If Python is so grumpy about types,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.580
 then we should be able to ask it about types.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.500
 So it turns out that there is inside Python

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.900
 a built-in function called type, T-Y-P-E.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.720
 So we can pass into type.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.580
 So the syntax is calling a built-in function named type.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.560
 Parenthesis is the parameter that we're passing to it.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.740
 We're saying hey, hello, tell me something

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.820
 about the type of the variable E-E-E-E.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.100
 And so this is a function.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.300
 The parentheses are part of the function call

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.780
 and it says oh, that would be of class string.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.580
 And then we can pass in a constant

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.420
 and it says hey, what about hello?

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.060
 The string hello, it's like oh, that's a string too.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:19.980
 What about a one?

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:20.900
 Well, that's an integer.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.240
 And so we are asking Python through the type function

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.380
 what the type of either a variable or a constant is.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.260
 And there are even several types of numbers

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.180
 and we'll even see Booleans and others later.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:38.340
 Like one with no decimal, that's an integer number.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.820
 98.6 with a decimal, that's a floating point number.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.820
 And so constants can be both integer and floating point.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.780
 And I'm just asking over and over and over again

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.020
 what is the type of, what's in X-X-X,

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.740
 what's the type of what's in temp,

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:57.340
 and what's the type of the constant one?

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.740
 And what's the type of 1.0?

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.900
 You can also use a set of built-in functions

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.820
 like float and int to convert from one to another.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.820
 And so this basically says I wanna convert, oops,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:16.720
 let's go back, I wanna convert 99 to a floating point number.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.860
 So this is a function and it's participating in this plus,

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:22.460
 but before it can finish the plus,

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.660
 it turns this into a 99.0.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.340
 The difference between 99 as an integer and 99.0

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.820
 is that it's a floating point number.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.840
 And that actually turns this computation

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.860
 as it looks to the left and looks to the right,

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.220
 says oh, I've got a floating point number on one side,

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.020
 an integer on the other side,

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.180
 and so I'm gonna make my calculation overall

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.500
 be a floating point calculation.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.020
 I can also pass into the float function,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.340
 I can say take this variable i,

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.780
 which has a 42, also an integer,

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:52.880
 and then give me back a floating point.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.300
 So that'll be 42.0, pass that into f,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.540
 we print it out, and it is indeed 42.0,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.620
 and it's a float.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.820
 And so it knows the type and value in any variable.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.300
 This is an integer of value 42.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.460
 This is a float of value 42.0.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:15.180
 Integer division in Python 2 was kinda weird,

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.780
 and it was actually one of the big things

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.420
 that they changed between Python 2 and Python 3.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:20.740
 This is a Python 3 course,

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.760
 so we're not worried about that too much.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.860
 What's nice about integer division in Python 3

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:28.340
 is it always produces a floating point result.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.980
 And that means that Python 3's division is more predictable,

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.060
 and it works more like a calculator.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:37.300
 So in this case, I mean, you can go back

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.720
 and look at my Python 2 lectures

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.900
 and see how crazy it was in Python 2.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.900
 10 divided by 2 is 5.0, and the weird thing here

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.340
 is these are both integers, but the division

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.400
 forces the result of the calculation

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.740
 to be a floating point number.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.700
 And this, you know, 10 over 2 could be 5,

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:57.660
 but 9 over 2 is 4.5, and so that is accurate.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.120
 In old Python 2, that would give us back 4,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.880
 which is completely unpredictable and weird.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.060
 The same with 99 over 100.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.980
 As you would expect, if this were a calculator,

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.900
 you'd get 0.99.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.300
 Actually, what you'll get in Python 2 is zero,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.580
 because it would round it down.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.140
 It doesn't round at all, it truncates it.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.100
 So 99 over 100 is 0.99, and then it truncates it to zero.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.200
 That's Python 2.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.840
 We're not talking about Python 2.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.860
 There's a good reason we're not talking about Python 2.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.300
 Welcome to Python 3.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.620
 Of course, if there are floating point on either side,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.260
 the result is still a floating point, floating point,

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.020
 and the result is still a floating point.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.180
 So integer division produces a floating result

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.860
 in Python 3.0, not in Python 2.0.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.140
 That is an improvement in Python 3.0.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.100
 And that's why we're recording these lectures.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.700
 I have a whole great set of lectures about Python 2,

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:50.220
 and now I'm gonna have a great set of lectures

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.480
 about Python 3.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.900
 Welcome to Python 3.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.540
 Okay, so we've been talking about converting

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:57.700
 from integer to floating point,

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.380
 but you can also convert from string to integer

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:01.780
 or string to floating point.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:06.360
 And so here we start out with a little string value.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.280
 Now it only works for strings that are made of digits.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.780
 So quote one, two, three, quote is not an integer.

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:15.060
 It is a three-character string that has one, two, three

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.600
 as the characters in that string,

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:19.140
 which is very different than 123.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.500
 We say, what is the type of this?

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:22.780
 It's a string.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:24.460
 We say, let's add one to it.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.660
 And it says, can't convert int to string,

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.020
 so that blows up, right?

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.260
 'Cause this is a string.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:31.300
 It looks to both sides.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.920
 String plus an integer, not good, okay?

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.760
 But we can convert this.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:38.360
 We can call the int function,

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.220
 which is like the float function, and pass a string in.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.540
 So it says, hey, take this and turn it into an integer.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:48.700
 So take the input of sval, which is the string one, two, three,

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.480
 and give me back an integer representation of that,

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.040
 which is going to be 123.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.700
 So we say, what kind of thing do we get back?

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.220
 Well, we got back an integer.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.180
 We can now add one to it and get 124.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:03.060
 And so you have to manage the type of things,

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.520
 and you can convert from one type to another.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:08.620
 Now, int is not magic.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:09.940
 If you send something into it,

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.000
 a string that doesn't consist of digits,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.220
 then you're gonna end up with another error.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.580
 Invalid literal for integer with base 10,

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:18.740
 blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:19.700
 So it's really complaining.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:21.440
 It says, I want these to be numbers here,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:22.840
 and you just gave me letters.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.220
 So that's going to cause this to fail.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:28.840
 Another thing that we're gonna do with variables

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:32.060
 is just like the print function takes something,

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:35.580
 a list of things, in this case, a string, comma, a variable,

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.020
 and then prints some output in the program,

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:39.460
 the opposite of that is input.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:42.020
 Actually, input generally happens before output.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:43.860
 Input is a built-in function,

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.780
 and we pass to it a prompt,

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:49.060
 a string of text that's going to be printed out for the user,

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.900
 and then it stops and waits.

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:52.500
 So it says, who are you?

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.520
 And then right here, it just sits,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.380
 waiting for us to type something.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:57.740
 So we type, blah, blah, blah, blah,

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:59.700
 and then hit the Enter key, right?

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:00.820
 We hit the Enter key,

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:04.620
 and then this text ends up in this variable.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.620
 So this is an assignment statement

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.460
 that chuck is the result of the input call,

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.560
 gets copied into the nam variable.

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:16.620
 So let's do that again.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:18.420
 It's evaluating assignment statement.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.660
 Remember, it's kind of this way,

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:22.700
 or you can think of it as just this right side first.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:26.140
 It writes this out, writes that out,

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.780
 then it waits, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait,

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.940
 until we hit the Enter and takes this chuck,

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.540
 and that becomes the result of this input,

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:38.900
 which is then assigned into nam.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.400
 Now then we go sequentially to the next line.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.780
 It prints out welcome, comma, contents of the variable nam.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.660
 Now this one, this comma here,

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.900
 actually does put the space in here automatically,

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.800
 so it says welcome space chuck.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:53.800
 So it pulls the, there's no space in chuck,

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:56.140
 just the ch-u-c-k.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.640
 And so print can take more than one thing,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:13:59.700
 separated by commas.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.860
 Matter of fact, print can have, you know,

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.260
 a whole bunch, oops, come back, come back, come back.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:08.740
 Print can have comma, comma, comma,

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:10.260
 parentheses, as many as you like.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:11.180
 Everything you've seen up to now

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:12.320
 is kind of one thing in the print,

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:15.260
 but that doesn't mean the print only can do one thing.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.620
 So I've talked about variables.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:18.800
 We've talked about constants.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:19.820
 We've talked about input.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:20.700
 We've talked about output.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.540
 And now it is time to write our first meaningful program.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:28.700
 And so this program has to do with those of you

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.020
 who have traveled internationally.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.560
 If you traveled to United States

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:34.020
 and you traveled outside the United States,

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.920
 you notice that there is an elevator convention

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:38.620
 that is different inside the United States.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:41.660
 In the United States, the walk in the ground floor

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.340
 in the elevator, that's one.

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.380
 And if you walk in the ground floor in Europe

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.860
 or many other places in the world,

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.940
 then the elevator is zero.

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.540
 So we have written a small app

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:51.580
 that we're gonna put on the App Store

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:56.580
 and get wealthy with called Elevator Floor Conversion App.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:59.620
 And it's gonna ask us, we're in Europe and we're lost,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.880
 and you say, well, what floor would this be

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.380
 if I was in the United States of America?

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.660
 And so here's, we have to read the floor

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:10.780
 that we are in Europe, and then we're going to convert it

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:12.500
 to a US floor, and then we're gonna print it out.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:17.500
 This is very silly, but it is a pure essential program

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:22.580
 that has input, does some kind of task on that input,

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.140
 and then produces some output,

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:28.780
 which is useful for some value of useful, okay?

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.100
 So let's take a look at how we combine everything

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:32.560
 that we've learned in this lecture,

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.320
 input, processing, and output.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.180
 It's a three-line program,

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:38.080
 but it's sort of the beginning

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:40.340
 of something that programs do, okay?

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.060
 You're gonna do lots of programs that do this.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.540
 So here we go.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.460
 Program starts, we do the input, side effect,

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.260
 it prints out this and then waits.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:53.180
 We type in zero, that comes back here,

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.700
 and the zero, which is a string.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:57.460
 Input gives you back a string.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.620
 It doesn't give you back a number.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:00.780
 It's a little different in Python 2,

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.820
 but in Python 3, input gives you a string.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.280
 So quote zero, quote, which is what we typed here.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:07.300
 We didn't type the quotes, it's a string.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.000
 It gets stored in the imp variable.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.220
 Then we move to the next statement,

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.460
 and on this right-hand side,

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:16.040
 we convert that string variable to an integer,

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.900
 so that becomes the integer zero.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:21.220
 We add one to it, and then that becomes one,

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:23.260
 and then we assign that into USF.

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:27.300
 I've named this variable United States Floor, right?

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:30.180
 So imp is the input and USF, that's mnemonic.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.860
 It doesn't know anything about elevators,

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.800
 it's just I picked a variable that was quite friendly.

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:40.860
 And so at this point, USF has the United States Floor

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.700
 that's equivalent to the European Floor,

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:45.400
 and then I just fall down and I do a print statement.

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:48.460
 Print out USFloor, USFloor, comma,

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:49.900
 that's this space right here,

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.020
 and then whatever the contents of the USFloor variable is.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:55.500
 And you could see that I could write this on four,

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:56.700
 and it would say three.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.900
 I could write this and say seven, and it would say six.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:01.260
 This is an amazing program.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:05.440
 It converts floors in a European numbering scheme.

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:08.220
 Wait, actually no, I got that wrong.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.780
 Hang on, let me clear this.

